            <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Teachers represent the largest single employee cohort in West Bengal. Under the 8th CPC working factor of 2.28, primary starting basic will rise from ₹28,900 to <strong>₹65,892 per month</strong>, while PG teachers will reach <strong>₹81,624 per month</strong>.</p>
            <span class="cadre-footer">Primary (ROPA 2019): ₹28,900 | 8th CPC (2.28x): <strong>₹65,892</strong></span>
          </div>

          <div class="cadre-box police">
            <h4>Kolkata Police & West Bengal Police</h4>
            <div class="cadre-badge">Law Enforcement & Armed Battalions</div>
            <p><strong>Covered Ranks:</strong> Constables (Level 6: ₹22,700), Assistant Sub-Inspectors (Level 8: ₹27,100), Sub-Inspectors (Level 11: ₹33,400), and Inspectors (Level 14: ₹42,600).</p>
            <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Police personnel draw specialized uniform, risk, and ration allowances in addition to baseline pay. Constables will see baseline pay expand to <strong>₹51,756</strong> under the 2.28 factor.</p>
            <span class="cadre-footer">Constable (ROPA 2019): ₹22,700 | 8th CPC (2.28x): <strong>₹51,756</strong></span>
          </div>

          <div class="cadre-box wbcs">
            <h4>West Bengal Civil Service (WBCS Executive)</h4>
            <div class="cadre-badge">State Administrative Leadership</div>
            <p><strong>Covered Ranks:</strong> Deputy Magistrate & Deputy Collector / BDO (Level 16: ₹56,100), SDO (Level 17: ₹67,300), and Additional District Magistrate / ADM (Level 19: ₹95,100).</p>
            <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Direct recruits through the Public Service Commission (PSCWB) manage sub-divisional governance, block development, and district revenue administration.</p>
            <span class="cadre-footer">WBCS Entry: ₹56,100 | 8th CPC (2.28x): <strong>₹1,27,908</strong></span>
          </div>

          <div class="cadre-box health">
            <h4>West Bengal Health Services (WBHS)</h4>
            <div class="cadre-badge">Medical Officers & Nursing Cadres</div>
            <p><strong>Covered Ranks:</strong> Medical Officers (Level 16: ₹56,100 plus NPA), Staff Nurses Gr-II (Level 9: ₹28,900), and Nursing Sisters (Level 10: ₹32,100).</p>
            <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Doctors draw Non-Practicing Allowance (NPA at 20% of basic pay), creating substantial gross remuneration expansions under the new matrix.</p>
            <span class="cadre-footer">Medical Officer: ₹56,100 | 8th CPC (2.28x): <strong>₹1,27,908</strong></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>House Rent Allowance (HRA) Rules in West Bengal: The 12% Ceiling Model</h2>
        <p>
          Unlike the Central Government three-tier classification (Class X 27%, Class Y 18%, Class Z 9%), House Rent Allowance in West Bengal operates under a <strong>uniform percentage rule</strong> across the entire state:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The ROPA HRA Formula & Ceiling Limit:</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Admissible HRA</strong> = 12% &times; Basic Pay, subject to a statutory maximum ceiling of <strong>₹12,000 per month</strong>.
          </p>
          <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
            <li><strong>Uniform State Applicability:</strong> Whether an employee is posted in the Kolkata Metropolitan Area (KMA) or in a remote rural gram panchayat in Purulia or Cooch Behar, the HRA rate is identically fixed at 12% of basic pay.</li>
            <li><strong>Spousal Allotment Restrictions:</strong> If both husband and wife are state government employees residing in the same accommodation, both can draw HRA provided their combined HRA does not exceed the maximum prescribed ceiling of ₹12,000 per month. If either spouse is allotted official government quarters, neither is entitled to draw HRA.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Pension & General Provident Fund (GPF) Framework in West Bengal</h2>
        <p>
          Retirement compensation in West Bengal carries exceptional policy significance due to its pension architecture:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Continuity of the Old Pension Scheme (OPS):</strong> Unlike the Central Government and most other states that transitioned all post-2004 recruits into the National Pension System (NPS), West Bengal maintained the defined-benefit <strong>Old Pension Scheme (OPS) with General Provident Fund (GPF)</strong> for regular state government employees for an extended period. Employees contribute a minimum of 6% of their basic pay to GPF without market risk deductions.</li>
          <li><strong>Revision for State Pensioners:</strong> Over six lakh state pensioners and family pensioners will see their baseline monthly pension multiplied by the approved fitment factor. The minimum pension floor in West Bengal will rise from ₹8,500 under ROPA 2019 to approximately <strong>₹19,380 per month</strong> under a 2.28 factor.</li>
          <li><strong>Fixed Medical Allowance (MA):</strong> Pensioners and active employees receive a monthly Medical Allowance (currently ₹500 per month), which employee federations have urged the government to expand to ₹1,500–₹2,000 per month to keep pace with geriatric medicine inflation.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Digital Payroll Integration: The IFMS West Bengal Network</h2>
        <p>
          The Government of West Bengal administers public wage disbursements through its centralized financial technology platform:
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>Integrated Financial Management System (IFMS West Bengal - wbifms.gov.in)</strong> managed by the Finance Department automates electronic salary processing (e-Bantan), monthly pay slips (e-Pradan), annual 3% vertical increment credits, CAS / Modified CAS career progressions (at 8, 16, and 25 years), and GPF accounting across all state treasuries.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the next ROPA notification is officially gazetted, revised basic pay matrices and retrospective arrear calculations will be automated within the electronic IFMS network, ensuring direct-benefit electronic bank transfers across all 23 districts of West Bengal.
        </p>
      </section>

